## 1. Introduction

Breast cancer exhibits profound molecular heterogeneity spanning chromosomal copy-number alterations (CNAs), somatic point mutations, and transcriptional state transitions. While clinicopathological indices such as the Nottingham Prognostic Index (`NPI`), TNM stage, and immunohistochemical receptor profiling (`ER`, `PR`, `HER2`) guide routine adjuvant treatment decisions, patients within identical clinical categories frequently experience divergent survival trajectories.

Commercial transcriptomic assays—including the 21-gene `Oncotype DX` recurrence score and 50-gene `PAM50` intrinsic subtyping panel—have improved risk estimation in early-stage hormone-receptor-positive disease, but they capture only bulk mRNA abundance and omit upstream DNA-level structural drivers such as `ERBB2`, `CDK4/6`, and `PIK3CA` amplifications or `TP53` and `KMT2C` loss-of-function mutations. Conversely, recent multi-omics machine learning benchmarks demonstrate that combining complementary genomic and transcriptomic layers improves prognostic calibration—provided that cross-platform batch effects, pseudogene artifacts, and training-to-test data leakage are rigorously controlled.

Here, we present an end-to-end, leakage-free three-omics prognostic modeling and translational validation framework trained on TCGA-BRCA ($n = 994$) and externally validated in METABRIC ($n = 1,865$) and GEO GSE20685 ($n = 327$). We directly benchmark three-layer multi-omics integration (`CNA + SNV + mRNA/Pathway`) against unimodal baselines, clinical staging, and re-trained `Oncotype DX` and `PAM50` gene panels, while evaluating receptor-subgroup generalizability, Shapley Additive exPlanations (`SHAP`), and 5-year clinical Decision Curve Analysis (`DCA`).

