### 3.2 Zero-Leakage Cutoff Stratification, Clinical Independence, and Receptor Subgroups
Applying the locked `TCGA-Train` median risk cutoff (`-0.0077`) directly to external METABRIC patients separated High-Risk ($n = 1,246$) and Low-Risk ($n = 619$) cohorts with highly significant overall survival divergence (**log-rank $p = 3.06 \times 10^{-19}$**, **Figure 2**). In multivariable Cox regression across all $1,865$ METABRIC patients (**Figure 3**), the standardized molecular risk score remained an independent prognostic determinant (**$\text{HR} = 1.151$ per SD**, 95% CI: $1.082\text{--}1.224$, **$p = 7.48 \times 10^{-6}$**) alongside age ($\text{HR} = 1.470$ per 10 years, $p = 1.57 \times 10^{-41}$), `NPI` ($\text{HR} = 1.328$, $p = 1.89 \times 10^{-22}$), and `ER-negative` status ($\text{HR} = 1.346$, $p = 0.0039$).

Subgroup Cox analysis across clinical receptor subtypes in METABRIC (**Table 2**) confirmed robust prognostic stratification within both **ER+/HER2- Luminal** breast cancer ($n = 1,159$, 680 events; **$\text{HR} = 1.342$ per SD**, 95% CI: $1.239\text{--}1.454$, **$p = 4.47 \times 10^{-13}$**) and **Triple-Negative Breast Cancer (TNBC)** ($n = 299$, 159 events; **$\text{HR} = 1.308$ per SD**, 95% CI: $1.119\text{--}1.529$, **$p = 7.35 \times 10^{-4}$**). In contrast, stratification was attenuated in the **HER2-Positive** subgroup ($n = 407$, $\text{HR} = 1.096$, $p = 0.149$), consistent with the pre-trastuzumab historical treatment era of METABRIC where `ERBB2` amplification dominated clinical outcomes.

