## 4. Discussion

By integrating GISTIC2 focal copy-number alterations (`ERBB2`, `CDK4`, `CDK6`, `PIK3CA`), somatic driver mutations (`TP53`, `PIK3CA`, `KMT2C`, `AKT1`), and sample-normalized protein-coding transcriptomics across $3,186$ breast cancer patients, this study establishes a reproducible, zero-leakage multi-omics prognostic framework that outperforms re-trained `Oncotype DX` and `PAM50` gene panels in external validation.

Critically, subgroup stratification demonstrates that the 3-omics signature resolves high-risk biology not only in `ER+/HER2-` luminal tumors ($\text{HR} = 1.342$, $p = 4.47 \times 10^{-13}$) but also in `Triple-Negative Breast Cancer` ($\text{HR} = 1.308$, $p = 7.35 \times 10^{-4}$), a high-mortality subtype where commercial luminal proliferation assays lack clinical utility. Mechanistically, the convergence of `CDK4/6` copy-number gain, `E2F` target hyperactivation, `PIK3CA/AKT1` alterations, late estrogen-response loss, and `IFN-$\gamma$`/inflammatory depletion defines an endocrine-resistant, immune-cold phenotype that may benefit from combined `CDK4/6` or `PI3K/AKT` inhibition rather than endocrine monotherapy.

